  "result": "I've planned all eight laws as 11 blueprint nodes, and the validator accepts every one. Five of them can be coded now. The other six carry gaps, so those laws stay blocked until you decide the gaps.\n\n**Ready to code:**\n- `NRS_484B_403_3` (no U-turn in a business district) and `NRS_484B_407` (no turning around on a curve or near a crest without 500 ft of sight). Both read the turn with the already-proposed `drv.motion.u_turn` concept.\n- `NRS_484B_410_o1` and `_o2`: two nodes because the law forbids two things, starting a halted vehicle on a highway and entering a highway. Each is allowed only when no other road user is on course to come within a set clearance within a set time.\n- `NRS_484B_413_3`: a vehicle that stops or slows sharply with another vehicle close behind must have shown its brake lamps or a hand signal at or just before that moment.\n\n**Blocked by gaps:**\n- **NRS-484B-440-1** (three nodes: no stopping on the paved part when there is room off it; leave a passing width; keep 200 ft of clear view). The law exempts disabled vehicles, and nothing in the vocabulary can tell whether a vehicle is disabled, so I filed the same \"disabled vehicle\" gap as the earlier pending gap G19.\n- **NRS-484B-450-1a/1c/1e** (no halting on a sidewalk, inside an intersection, or on a crosswalk). The exception \"in compliance with law\" can't be written: a formula can only refer to one named rule, not \"some law requires this halt\". The validator insisted on carrying the pending gap G22 for 1c. I filed the same gap on 1a and 1e because the words are identical. A left turner waiting for oncoming traffic inside an intersection is the case that would be judged wrongly without it.\n\n**Decisions for you:**\n- **G22:** another node planned this iteration (`LVMC_11_52_130_A11`) maps the same \"in compliance with law\" words with plain device checks and no gap. Whichever way you decide G22, it should apply to both.\n- **NRS-484B-410:** the exception \"except when stopping, standing or parking where no parking is permitted\" reads oddly. I planned it as written, applied to the halt just before starting or entering; the source wording may be worth checking.\n- **New parameters** awaiting your review: `p.crest_near_reach`, `p.sudden_decel_min`, `p.rear_signal_reach`, `p.stop_signal_lead_window`, `p.offroad_refuge_reach`, `p.free_passage_width`.\n\nI wrote three files to the batch-21 folder: `blueprint.jsonl`, `PLAN.md` (one paragraph per node with what would reverse each decision), and an empty `aggregates.jsonl`.",
